  <figcaption>Parameter comparison between Inkling-Small and Inkling.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Compared to Inkling, Inkling-Small achieves comparable performance with much less compute. Across agentic tool use (Terminal-Bench 2.1), reasoning (HLE text-only, no tools), and instruction following (IFBench) benchmarks, Inkling-Small is more efficient than Inkling and competitive with other models in its weight class. Furthermore, its <a href="/news/introducing-inkling/#controllable-thinking-effort">variable thinking effort</a> lets users easily adapt it to target their use case, balancing cost and performance.</p>
<style>

  /* This figure's caption swaps with the axis toggle, so its label needs the "1a"
     / "1b" suffix a CSS counter cannot produce. Suppress the counter-driven label
     and put the number on each swappable caption instead, via the same attr()
     mechanism as .audio-comparison-figure[data-figure-number] in blog-post.css.
     Every other caption on the post uses the site-wide pseudo-element. */
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-figure-caption::before {
    content: none;
  }

  #main.post article.content .se-cap[data-figure-number]::before {
    content: "Figure " attr(data-figure-number) ": ";
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Axis toggle — inkling-post demo-tabs style (pill row + sliding indicator). */
  /* Symmetric block margin: the three-value shorthand here used to zero the
     bottom, and since paragraphs carry no top margin the caption collided with
     the following copy. */
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher { margin: 2.2rem 0; }
  #main.post article.content .se-toggle-row { align-items: center; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.55rem; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tabs { --demo-tab-indicator-opacity: 0; --demo-tab-width: 0px; --demo-tab-height: 0px; --demo-tab-x: 0px; --demo-tab-y: 0px; align-items: center; display: inline-flex; gap: 0.45rem; isolation: isolate; justify-content: center; position: relative; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tabs::before { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 10%, var(--bg) 90%); border-radius: 999px; content: ""; height: var(--demo-tab-height); left: 0; opacity: var(--demo-tab-indicator-opacity); pointer-events: none; position: absolute; top: 0; width: var(--demo-tab-width); transform: translate3d(var(--demo-tab-x), var(--demo-tab-y), 0); z-index: 0; transition: transform 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), width 240ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), height 240ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), opacity 120ms ease; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tab { appearance: none; background: transparent; border: 0; border-radius: 999px; color: var(--fg2); cursor: pointer; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; line-height: 1; padding: 0.62rem 0.9rem; position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tab:hover, #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tab:focus-visible { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 7%, var(--bg) 93%); }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tab[aria-selected="true"] { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 10%, var(--bg) 90%); color: var(--fg); }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .demo-tabs.demo-tabs-enhanced .demo-tab[aria-selected="true"] { background: transparent; }
  #main.post article.content .se-panel[hidden], #main.post article.content .se-cap[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Break the whole switcher out of the reading column and center it in the
     viewport, then force the chart to actually fill that width.

     1280px, not the 1171px the other figures use, because this figure is a real
     <figure> element and the others are <div>s inside one. blog-post.css guards
     figures below 1280 with

       @media (max-width: 1279px) {
         #main.post article.content figure:not(.half-width) {
           width: 100%; max-width: 100%; transform: none;
         }
       }

     which outweighs the selector below (same ids and classes, one more element),
     so between 1171 and 1279 it stripped the width and the translateX while
     leaving left: 50% behind -- shunting the figure half the reading column to
     the right and ~69px off the viewport. Starting the breakout where the guard
     ends is what keeps the two from disagreeing. Below it the figure sits in the
     column and .ce-scroll scrolls, exactly as it does on a phone. */
  @media (min-width: 1280px) {
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher {
      position: relative;
      left: 50%;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
      width: min(1200px, 100vw - 48px);
      max-width: none;
      margin-left: 0;
      margin-right: 0;
    }

    /* Only at the breakout does the chart stretch to fill the wider box. Below it
       the figure's own min-width has to survive, or the three panels squeeze to
       the viewport instead of scrolling -- which shrank the axis type to ~5px on a
       phone. Scoping these to the breakout is what lets .ce-scroll scroll, the
       same way the Inkling post's effort-curve figure behaves. */
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-scroll,
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-chart,
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .intelligence-interactivity-chart,
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-svg,
    #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-figure svg { min-width: 0; }
  }
  #main.post article.content .se-panels,
  #main.post article.content .se-panel { width: 100%; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-figure { margin: 0 !important; max-width: 100% !important; width: 100% !important; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-scroll,
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-chart,
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .intelligence-interactivity-chart { max-width: none; width: 100%; margin: 0; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .intelligence-interactivity-legend { max-width: 860px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-svg,
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-switcher .ce-figure svg { width: 100%; height: auto; }
  #main.post article.content .small-efficiency-figure-caption { max-width: 660px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }
</style>
<script src="/news/inkling-small/chart-tooltips-v1.js" defer></script>
<figure class="small-efficiency-switcher" data-se-switch>
<div class="se-toggle-row">
  <div class="demo-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Efficiency chart axis">
    <button class="demo-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="se-tab-flops" aria-controls="se-panel-flops" aria-selected="true" data-se-tab="flops">TFLOPs</button>
    <button class="demo-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="se-tab-cost" aria-controls="se-panel-cost" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-se-tab="cost">Cost</button>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="se-panels">
  <div class="se-panel" id="se-panel-flops" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="se-tab-flops" data-se-panel="flops">
<style>
  .ce-figure {
    --ce-inklingsmall: #8F3F71;
    --ce-inkling: #0155BF;
    /* One grey for every comparison model, matching Figure 3's --cq-peer.
       They are context for our two sweeps, not ten identities to track; each
       point still names itself on hover and focus via data-ii-tooltip-title. */
    --ce-peer: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg4) 50%, var(--bg) 50%);
    --ce-pareto: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg4) 26%, var(--bg) 74%);
    color: var(--fg);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: 1480px;
    width: 100%;
  }

  /* Figure "breakout" past the reading column on large screens (house style),
     centred on the column via a symmetric negative margin. */
  @media (min-width: 1171px) {
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure {
      margin-left: calc((100% - 1480px) / 2);
      margin-right: 0;
      max-width: none;
      width: 1480px;
    }
  }

  /* Narrow screens scroll rather than shrinking to illegibility (mirrors
     effort-curves.html and the benchmark tables). */
  #main.post article.content .ce-scroll {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    overflow-y: hidden;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    width: 100%;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
  }

  .ce-scroll::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 6px; }
  .ce-scroll::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg4) 45%, var(--bg) 55%);
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .ce-chart { min-width: 760px; }
  .ce-figure svg { min-width: 760px; }
  .ce-figure polyline { fill: none; }

  .ce-pareto {
    stroke: var(--ce-pareto);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-width: 9;
  }

  .ce-line {
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-width: 1.7;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .ce-hero-line { stroke-width: 2.6; }

  /* Dash patterns follow the landing-page spider chart, so a series stays
     identifiable without relying on hue alone. */

  .ce-inklingsmall { stroke: var(--ce-inklingsmall); fill: var(--ce-inklingsmall); }
  .ce-inkling { stroke: var(--ce-inkling); fill: var(--ce-inkling); }
  /* Fill only: .ii-point-marker in blog-post.css gives every marker a 2px --bg
     stroke, and that halo is what keeps overlapping comparison points legible now
     that they are all one colour. Overriding the stroke here would merge clusters
     into blobs. (Our two series do set a stroke, deliberately — their markers sit
     on a line of the same colour.) */
  .ce-peer { fill: var(--ce-peer); }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-label {
    fill: var(--ce-inklingsmall);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 500;
    paint-order: stroke;
    stroke: var(--bg);
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-width: 3.5px;
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-leader {
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ce-inklingsmall) 55%, var(--bg) 45%);
    stroke-width: 1;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-legend { font-size: 12px; }
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-panel-title { font-size: 14px; }
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-ticks text,
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-axis-label { font-size: 12.5px; }
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-ylabel { font-size: 12.5px; }

  .ce-legend-meta { color: var(--fg4); }

  /* Legend swatch: a line in the series' own dash pattern with a marker on it. */
  .ce-swatch {
    display: inline-block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: 12px;
    position: relative;
    width: 24px;
  }

  .ce-swatch::before {
    background: var(--ce-swatch-color);
    content: "";
    height: 2px;
    left: 0;
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: 5px;
  }

  /* Centred by transform, not by hardcoded offsets: box-sizing is content-box, so
     the 8px dot plus its 1px border is a 10px box and the old left/top (computed
     for an 8px box) sat it 1px low and 1px right of the line's centre. */
  .ce-swatch::after {
    background: var(--ce-swatch-color);
    border: 1px solid var(--bg);
    border-radius: 50%;
    content: "";
    height: 8px;
    left: 50%;
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: 8px;
  }

  .ce-swatch--dash-7::before {
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,
      var(--ce-swatch-color) 0 6px, transparent 6px 10px);
  }

  .ce-swatch--dash-2::before {
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,
      var(--ce-swatch-color) 0 2.5px, transparent 2.5px 6px);
  }

  .ce-swatch--hero::before { height: 2.6px; top: 4.7px; }



  /* Reveal: the hero sweep wipes itself in left-to-right, the comparison series
     and the frontier ribbon fade up under it. Markers are handled by the shared
     ii-point animation in blog-post.css.

     The wipe is a clip-path rather than the usual pathLength + stroke-dasharray
     trick, because that trick is incompatible with vector-effect:
     non-scaling-stroke -- the two together left the line permanently cut off at
     roughly half its length. The vertical -20% keeps the round stroke caps from
     being clipped. With no class applied the line is simply fully drawn, so a
     figure that never reveals still renders correctly. */

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-pending .ce-hero-series,
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-pending .ce-base-series,
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-pending .ce-pareto,
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-pending .ce-labels {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-pending .ce-hero-series {
    clip-path: inset(-20% 100% -20% 0%);
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ce-hero-series {
    animation: ce-series-in 520ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.75, 0.25, 1) both;
    animation-delay: var(--ce-series-delay, 0ms);
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ce-base-series {
    animation: ce-fade-in 420ms ease-out both;
    animation-delay: var(--ce-series-delay, 0ms);
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ce-pareto {
    animation: ce-fade-in 420ms ease-out both;
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ce-labels {
    animation: ce-fade-in 380ms ease-out both;
    animation-delay: 620ms;
  }

  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ii-point,
  #main.post article.content .ce-figure.is-plot-visible .ii-point-marker {
    animation-duration: 260ms;
  }

  @keyframes ce-series-in {
    from { opacity: 0; clip-path: inset(-20% 100% -20% 0%); }
    to { opacity: 1; clip-path: inset(-20% 0% -20% 0%); }
  }

  @keyframes ce-fade-in {
    from { opacity: 0; }
    to { opacity: 1; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-hero-series,
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-base-series,
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-pareto,
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-labels {
      animation: none;
      clip-path: none;
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  /* Below the breakout the SVG is laid out at its 760px min-width and scrolls;
     at the breakout it fills min(1200px, 100vw - 48px). Scale the type by
     1200/760 so it renders at the same size either side of the breakpoint. (The
     previous values were computed against a 940px breakout that the switcher no
     longer uses, which left the mobile axis type undersized.)

     1279px, matching the switcher's `min-width: 1280px` breakout in index.md. It
     used to say 1170px, from when the breakout started at 1171px; once that moved
     to 1280 to stop colliding with blog-post.css's figure guard, 1171-1279 was
     left with neither the breakout nor this compensation -- the SVG laid out at
     760px against a 1332 viewBox (scale 0.571) with unscaled type, so panel
     titles rendered at 8px and axis labels at 7.1px. The two boundaries have to
     stay adjacent: every width that is not broken out needs the scale-up. */
  @media (max-width: 1279px) {
    /* !important on these two only: blog-post.css's max-width:640px block marks
       .ii-panel-title and .ii-axis-label !important (32px / 30px), sized for a
       figure that renders at container width. Same override the Inkling post's
       effort-curve figure needs. The tick rule there is not !important, so the
       figure-scoped selector wins on specificity alone. */
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-panel-title { font-size: 22.1px !important; }
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-axis-label { font-size: 19.7px !important; }
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ii-ticks text,
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-ylabel { font-size: 19.7px; }
    #main.post article.content .ce-figure .ce-label { font-size: 18.9px; }
  }
</style>
